<http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/CF12N420/>
        rdf:type               skos:Concept;
        dce:identifier         "SDN:P07::CF12N420";
        dc:date                "2009-07-06 15:41:29.0";
        dc:identifier          "SDN:P07::CF12N420";
        pav:authoredOn         "2009-07-06 15:41:29.0";
        pav:hasCurrentVersion  <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/CF12N420/1/>;
        pav:version            "1";
        void:inDataset         <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/.well-known/void>;
        owl:deprecated         "false";
        owl:sameAs             <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/standard_name/mole_concentration_of_aceto_nitrile_in_air/> , <http://mmisw.org/ont/cf/parameter/mole_concentration_of_aceto_nitrile_in_air>;
        owl:versionInfo        "1";
        skos:altLabel          "";
        skos:broader           <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P02/current/AAMN/>;
        skos:definition        "Mole concentration means number of moles per unit volume, also called \"molarity\", and is used in the construction mole_concentration_of_X_in_Y, where X is a material constituent of Y.  A chemical species denoted by X may be described by a single term such as 'nitrogen' or a phrase such as 'nox_expressed_as_nitrogen'. The chemical formula for aceto-nitrile is CH3CN. The IUPAC name for aceto-nitrile is ethanenitrile."@en;
        skos:notation          "SDN:P07::CF12N420";
        skos:note              "accepted"@en;
        skos:prefLabel         "mole_concentration_of_aceto_nitrile_in_air"@en;
        skos:related           <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P06/current/MLM3/> .

<http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/58FC3UBA/>
        rdf:type               skos:Concept;
        dce:identifier         "SDN:P07::58FC3UBA";
        dc:date                "2021-01-18 16:23:44.0";
        dc:identifier          "SDN:P07::58FC3UBA";
        pav:authoredOn         "2021-01-18 16:23:44.0";
        pav:hasCurrentVersion  <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/58FC3UBA/1/>;
        pav:version            "1";
        void:inDataset         <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/.well-known/void>;
        owl:deprecated         "false";
        owl:sameAs             <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/standard_name/to_direction_of_surface_downward_stress/> , <http://mmisw.org/ont/cf/parameter/to_direction_of_surface_downward_stress>;
        owl:versionInfo        "1";
        skos:altLabel          "";
        skos:definition        "The phrase \"to_direction\" is used in the construction X_to_direction and indicates the direction towards which the vector of X is headed. The direction is a bearing in the usual geographical sense, measured positive clockwise from due north. The surface called \"surface\" means the lower boundary of the atmosphere. \"Downward\" indicates a vector component which is positive when directed downward (negative upward). \"Surface stress\" means the shear stress (force per unit area) exerted by the wind at the surface. A downward stress is a downward flux of momentum. Over large bodies of water, wind stress can drive near-surface currents."@en;
        skos:notation          "SDN:P07::58FC3UBA";
        skos:note              "accepted"@en;
        skos:prefLabel         "to_direction_of_surface_downward_stress"@en;
        skos:related           <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P06/current/UAAA/> .

<http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/GWEZRUTL/>
        rdf:type               skos:Concept;
        dce:identifier         "SDN:P07::GWEZRUTL";
        dc:date                "2023-04-24 17:39:19.0";
        dc:identifier          "SDN:P07::GWEZRUTL";
        pav:authoredOn         "2023-04-24 17:39:19.0";
        pav:hasCurrentVersion  <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/GWEZRUTL/1/>;
        pav:version            "1";
        void:inDataset         <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/.well-known/void>;
        owl:deprecated         "false";
        owl:sameAs             <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/standard_name/cloud_type/> , <http://mmisw.org/ont/cf/parameter/cloud_type>;
        owl:versionInfo        "1";
        skos:altLabel          "";
        skos:definition        "A variable with the standard_name of cloud_type contains either strings which indicate the cloud type, or flags which can be translated to strings using flag_values and flag_meanings attributes."@en;
        skos:notation          "SDN:P07::GWEZRUTL";
        skos:note              "accepted"@en;
        skos:prefLabel         "cloud_type"@en;
        skos:related           <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P06/current/UUUU/> .

<http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/6JUAY160/>
        rdf:type               skos:Concept;
        dce:identifier         "SDN:P07::6JUAY160";
        dc:date                "2018-02-12 16:53:18.0";
        dc:identifier          "SDN:P07::6JUAY160";
        pav:authoredOn         "2018-02-12 16:53:18.0";
        pav:hasCurrentVersion  <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/6JUAY160/1/>;
        pav:version            "1";
        void:inDataset         <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/.well-known/void>;
        owl:deprecated         "false";
        owl:sameAs             <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/standard_name/integral_wrt_time_of_radioactivity_concentration_of_211Pb_in_air/> , <http://mmisw.org/ont/cf/parameter/integral_wrt_time_of_radioactivity_concentration_of_211Pb_in_air>;
        owl:versionInfo        "1";
        skos:altLabel          "";
        skos:definition        "The phrase \"integral_wrt_X_of_Y\" means int Y dX. The data variable should have an axis for X specifying the limits of the integral as bounds. The phrase \"wrt\" means \"with respect to\". \"Radioactivity\" means the number of radioactive decays of a material per second. \"Radioactivity concentration\" means radioactivity per unit volume of the medium. \"Pb\" means the element \"lead\" and \"211Pb\" is the isotope \"lead-211\" with a half-life of 2.51e-02 days."@en;
        skos:notation          "SDN:P07::6JUAY160";
        skos:note              "accepted"@en;
        skos:prefLabel         "integral_wrt_time_of_radioactivity_concentration_of_211Pb_in_air"@en;
        skos:related           <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P06/current/BSM3/> .

<http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/76LWI9UK/>
        rdf:type               skos:Concept;
        dce:identifier         "SDN:P07::76LWI9UK";
        dc:date                "2018-04-16 15:20:49.0";
        dc:identifier          "SDN:P07::76LWI9UK";
        pav:authoredOn         "2018-04-16 15:20:49.0";
        pav:hasCurrentVersion  <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/76LWI9UK/1/>;
        pav:version            "1";
        void:inDataset         <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/.well-known/void>;
        owl:deprecated         "false";
        owl:sameAs             <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/standard_name/surface_upward_mass_flux_of_methane_due_to_emission_from_wetland_biological_production/> , <http://mmisw.org/ont/cf/parameter/surface_upward_mass_flux_of_methane_due_to_emission_from_wetland_biological_production>;
        owl:versionInfo        "1";
        skos:altLabel          "";
        skos:definition        "The surface called \"surface\" means the lower boundary of the atmosphere. \"Upward\" indicates a vector component which is positive when directed upward (negative downward). In accordance with common usage in geophysical disciplines, \"flux\" implies per unit area, called \"flux density\" in physics. The chemical formula for methane is CH4. The mass is the total mass of the molecules. The phrase \"tendency_of_X\" means derivative of X with respect to time. The specification of a physical process by the phrase \"due_to_\" process means that the quantity named is a single term in a sum of terms which together compose the general quantity named by omitting the phrase. \"Emission\" means emission from a primary source located anywhere within the atmosphere, including at the lower boundary (i.e. the surface of the earth). \"Emission\" is a process entirely distinct from \"re-emission\" which is used in some standard names. Wetlands are areas where water covers the soil, or is present either at or near the surface of the soil all year or for varying periods of time during the year, including during the growing season. The precise conditions under which wetlands produce and consume methane can vary between models."@en;
        skos:notation          "SDN:P07::76LWI9UK";
        skos:note              "accepted"@en;
        skos:prefLabel         "surface_upward_mass_flux_of_methane_due_to_emission_from_wetland_biological_production"@en;
        skos:related           <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P06/current/KSP2/> .

<http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/QFY2YIPB/>
        rdf:type               skos:Concept;
        dce:identifier         "SDN:P07::QFY2YIPB";
        dc:date                "2024-12-16 16:23:22.0";
        dc:identifier          "SDN:P07::QFY2YIPB";
        pav:authoredOn         "2024-12-16 16:23:22.0";
        pav:hasCurrentVersion  <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/QFY2YIPB/2/>;
        pav:hasVersion         <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/QFY2YIPB/1/>;
        pav:version            "2";
        void:inDataset         <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/.well-known/void>;
        owl:deprecated         "false";
        owl:sameAs             <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/standard_name/toa_outgoing_longwave_flux_assuming_reference_mole_fraction_of_ozone_in_air/> , <http://mmisw.org/ont/cf/parameter/toa_outgoing_longwave_flux_assuming_reference_mole_fraction_of_ozone_in_air>;
        owl:versionInfo        "2";
        skos:altLabel          "";
        skos:definition        "A phrase assuming_condition indicates that the named quantity is the value which would obtain if all aspects of the system were unaltered except for the assumption of the circumstances specified by the condition. The term \"longwave\" means longwave radiation. The abbreviation \"toa\" means top of atmosphere. The TOA outgoing longwave flux is the upwelling thermal radiative flux, often called the \"outgoing longwave radiation\" or \"OLR\". In accordance with common usage in geophysical disciplines, \"flux\" implies per unit area, called \"flux density\" in physics. This 3D ozone field acts as a reference ozone field in a diagnostic call to the model's radiation scheme. It is expressed in terms of mole fraction of ozone in air. It may be observation-based or model-derived. It may be from any time period. By using the same ozone reference in the diagnostic radiation call in two model simulations and calculating differences between the radiative flux diagnostics from the prognostic call to the radiation scheme and the diagnostic call to the radiation scheme with the ozone reference, an instantaneous radiative forcing for ozone can be calculated."@en;
        skos:notation          "SDN:P07::QFY2YIPB";
        skos:note              "accepted"@en;
        skos:prefLabel         "toa_outgoing_longwave_flux_assuming_reference_mole_fraction_of_ozone_in_air"@en;
        skos:related           <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P06/current/UFAA/> .

