        owl:versionInfo        "1";
        skos:altLabel          "";
        skos:definition        "The surface called \"surface\" means the lower boundary of the atmosphere. \"Content\" indicates a quantity per unit area. \"Radioactivity\" means the number of radioactive decays of a material per second. \"Bi\" means the element \"bismuth\" and \"215Bi\" is the isotope \"bismuth-215\" with a half-life of 4.86e-03 days."@en;
        skos:notation          "SDN:P07::UWKRSRXF";
        skos:note              "accepted"@en;
        skos:prefLabel         "surface_radioactivity_content_of_215Bi"@en;
        skos:related           <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P06/current/BQSM/> .

<http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/UZBGMNUD/>
        rdf:type               skos:Concept;
        dce:identifier         "SDN:P07::UZBGMNUD";
        dc:date                "2013-11-08 14:08:46.0";
        dc:identifier          "SDN:P07::UZBGMNUD";
        pav:authoredOn         "2013-11-08 14:08:46.0";
        pav:hasCurrentVersion  <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/UZBGMNUD/1/>;
        pav:version            "1";
        void:inDataset         <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/.well-known/void>;
        owl:deprecated         "false";
        owl:sameAs             <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/standard_name/atmosphere_level_of_free_convection_wrt_surface/> , <http://mmisw.org/ont/cf/parameter/atmosphere_level_of_free_convection_wrt_surface>;
        owl:versionInfo        "1";
        skos:altLabel          "";
        skos:definition        "The level of free convection is the altitude where the temperature of the environment decreases faster than the moist adiabatic lapse rate of a saturated air parcel at the same level.  It is calculated by lifting a parcel of air dry adiabatically from the surface to the LCL (lifting condensation level), then moist adiabatically until the parcel temperature is equal to the ambient temperature."@en;
        skos:notation          "SDN:P07::UZBGMNUD";
        skos:note              "accepted"@en;
        skos:prefLabel         "atmosphere_level_of_free_convection_wrt_surface"@en;
        skos:related           <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P06/current/ULAA/> .

<http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/CF12N324/>
        rdf:type               skos:Concept;
        dce:identifier         "SDN:P07::CF12N324";
        dc:date                "2009-07-06 15:41:29.0";
        dc:identifier          "SDN:P07::CF12N324";
        pav:authoredOn         "2009-07-06 15:41:29.0";
        pav:hasCurrentVersion  <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/CF12N324/1/>;
        pav:version            "1";
        void:inDataset         <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/.well-known/void>;
        owl:deprecated         "false";
        owl:sameAs             <http://mmisw.org/ont/cf/parameter/mass_concentration_of_water_vapor_in_air> , <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/standard_name/mass_concentration_of_water_vapor_in_air/>;
        owl:versionInfo        "1";
        skos:altLabel          "";
        skos:broader           <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P02/current/CHUM/> , <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P64/current/G242/> , <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P04/current/G242/>;
        skos:definition        "Mass concentration means mass per unit volume and is used in the construction mass_concentration_of_X_in_Y, where X is a material constituent of Y.  A chemical species denoted by X may be described by a single term such as 'nitrogen' or a phrase such as 'nox_expressed_as_nitrogen'."@en;
        skos:notation          "SDN:P07::CF12N324";
        skos:note              "accepted"@en;
        skos:prefLabel         "mass_concentration_of_water_vapor_in_air"@en;
        skos:related           <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P06/current/UKMC/> .

<http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/AX74Z0HE/>
        rdf:type               skos:Concept;
        dce:identifier         "SDN:P07::AX74Z0HE";
        dc:date                "2026-03-16 11:27:13.0";
        dc:identifier          "SDN:P07::AX74Z0HE";
        pav:authoredOn         "2026-03-16 11:27:13.0";
        pav:hasCurrentVersion  <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/AX74Z0HE/1/>;
        pav:version            "1";
        void:inDataset         <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/.well-known/void>;
        owl:deprecated         "false";
        owl:sameAs             <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/standard_name/difference_between_change_in_soil_moisture_content_and_sum_of_estimated_contributions/>;
        owl:versionInfo        "1";
        skos:altLabel          "";
        skos:definition        "\"Water\" means water in all phases. \"Content\" indicates a quantity per unit area. The mass content of water in soil refers to the vertical integral from the surface down to those layer of the soil model which is the lowermost hydrological active layer. For the content between specified levels in the soil, standard names including \"content_of_soil_layer\" are used. This quantity is defined by the difference between the tendency of the soil moisture content over a specific time period (left hand side of soil water equation) and the sum of the sources and sinks of soil water over the same time period (right hand side of equation). A positive value means that the soil becomes falsely wetter in a model and a negative value means the soil becomes falsely dryer. It should be noted, that the difference has to be calculated excluding climatological layers where the temperature or the soil water content is kept to a climatological value or a value given by another soil layer. It is not possible to exactly summarize all contributions, as numerical discretizations lead to small inconsistencies to the analytical description of budgets. Thus, the contributions are \"the best estimate of contributions\"."@en;
        skos:notation          "SDN:P07::AX74Z0HE";
        skos:note              "accepted"@en;
        skos:prefLabel         "difference_between_change_in_soil_moisture_content_and_sum_of_estimated_contributions"@en;
        skos:related           <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P06/current/KMP2/> .

<http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/7X0DRNML/>
        rdf:type               skos:Concept;
        dce:identifier         "SDN:P07::7X0DRNML";
        dc:date                "2015-01-07 16:23:38.0";
        dc:identifier          "SDN:P07::7X0DRNML";
        pav:authoredOn         "2015-01-07 16:23:38.0";
        pav:hasCurrentVersion  <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/7X0DRNML/2/>;
        pav:hasVersion         <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/7X0DRNML/1/>;
        pav:version            "2";
        void:inDataset         <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/.well-known/void>;
        owl:deprecated         "false";
        owl:sameAs             <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/standard_name/tendency_of_atmosphere_mass_content_of_particulate_organic_matter_dry_aerosol_particles_expressed_as_carbon_due_to_emission/> , <http://mmisw.org/ont/cf/parameter/tendency_of_atmosphere_mass_content_of_particulate_organic_matter_dry_aerosol_particles_expressed_as_carbon_due_to_emission>;
        owl:versionInfo        "2";
        skos:altLabel          "";
        skos:definition        "\"Content\" indicates a quantity per unit area. The \"atmosphere content\" of a quantity refers to the vertical integral from the surface to the top of the atmosphere. For the content between specified levels in the atmosphere, standard names including \"content_of_atmosphere_layer\" are used. The phrase \"expressed_as\" is used in the construction A_expressed_as_B, where B is a chemical constituent of A. It means that the quantity indicated by the standard name is calculated solely with respect to the B contained in A, neglecting all other chemical constituents of A. \"Aerosol\" means the system of suspended liquid or solid particles in air (except cloud droplets) and their carrier gas, the air itself. The term \"particulate_organic_matter_dry_aerosol\" means all particulate organic matter dry aerosol except black carbon. It is the sum of primary_particulate_organic_matter_dry_aerosol and secondary_particulate_organic_matter_dry_aerosol. Aerosol particles take up ambient water (a process known as hygroscopic growth) depending on the relative humidity and the composition of the particles. \"Dry aerosol particles\" means aerosol particles without any water uptake. The specification of a physical process by the phrase \"due_to_\" process means that the quantity named is a single term in a sum of terms which together compose the general quantity named by omitting the phrase. \"Emission\" means emission from a primary source located anywhere within the atmosphere, including at the lower boundary (i.e. the surface of the earth). \"Emission\" is a process entirely distinct from \"re-emission\" which is used in some standard names. \"tendency_of_X\" means derivative of X with respect to time."@en;
        skos:notation          "SDN:P07::7X0DRNML";
        skos:note              "accepted"@en;
        skos:prefLabel         "tendency_of_atmosphere_mass_content_of_particulate_organic_matter_dry_aerosol_particles_expressed_as_carbon_due_to_emission"@en;
        skos:related           <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P06/current/KSP2/> .

<http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/N1DP5O1H/>
        rdf:type               skos:Concept;
        dce:identifier         "SDN:P07::N1DP5O1H";
        dc:date                "2018-04-16 15:20:49.0";
        dc:identifier          "SDN:P07::N1DP5O1H";
        pav:authoredOn         "2018-04-16 15:20:49.0";
        pav:hasCurrentVersion  <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/N1DP5O1H/1/>;
        pav:version            "1";
        void:inDataset         <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/.well-known/void>;
        owl:deprecated         "false";
        owl:sameAs             <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/standard_name/nitrogen_mass_flux_into_forestry_and_agricultural_products_due_to_anthropogenic_land_use_or_land_cover_change/> , <http://mmisw.org/ont/cf/parameter/nitrogen_mass_flux_into_forestry_and_agricultural_products_due_to_anthropogenic_land_use_or_land_cover_change>;
        owl:versionInfo        "1";
        skos:altLabel          "";
        skos:definition        "In accordance with common usage in geophysical disciplines, \"flux\" implies per unit area, called \"flux density\" in physics. Examples of \"forestry and agricultural products\" are paper, cardboard, furniture, timber for construction, biofuels and food for both humans and livestock. Models that simulate land use changes have one or more pools of nitrogen that represent these products in order to conserve nitrogen and allow its eventual release into the atmosphere, for example, when the products decompose in landfill sites. \"Anthropogenic\" means influenced, caused, or created by human activity. \"Anthropogenic land use change\" means human changes to land, excluding forest regrowth. It includes fires ignited by humans for the purpose of land use change and the processes of eventual disposal and decomposition of wood products such as paper, cardboard, furniture and timber for construction."@en;
        skos:notation          "SDN:P07::N1DP5O1H";
        skos:note              "accepted"@en;
        skos:prefLabel         "nitrogen_mass_flux_into_forestry_and_agricultural_products_due_to_anthropogenic_land_use_or_land_cover_change"@en;
        skos:related           <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P06/current/KSP2/> .

<http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/CFV16A40/>
        rdf:type               skos:Concept;
        dce:identifier         "SDN:P07::CFV16A40";
        dc:date                "2025-05-13 08:28:41.0";
        dc:identifier          "SDN:P07::CFV16A40";
        pav:authoredOn         "2025-05-13 08:28:41.0";
        pav:hasCurrentVersion  <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/CFV16A40/2/>;
        pav:hasVersion         <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/CFV16A40/1/>;
        pav:version            "2";
