<http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/CFSN0221/>
        rdf:type               skos:Concept;
        dce:identifier         "SDN:P07::CFSN0221";
        dc:date                "2021-01-18 16:23:44.0";
        dc:identifier          "SDN:P07::CFSN0221";
        pav:authoredOn         "2021-01-18 16:23:44.0";
        pav:hasCurrentVersion  <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/CFSN0221/2/>;
        pav:hasVersion         <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/CFSN0221/1/>;
        pav:version            "2";
        void:inDataset         <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/.well-known/void>;
        owl:deprecated         "false";
        owl:sameAs             <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/standard_name/surface_snow_sublimation_heat_flux/> , <http://mmisw.org/ont/cf/parameter/surface_snow_sublimation_heat_flux>;
        owl:versionInfo        "2";
        skos:altLabel          "";
        skos:broader           <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P04/current/G241/> , <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P64/current/G241/>;
        skos:definition        "Surface snow refers to the snow on the solid ground or on surface ice cover, but excludes, for example, falling snowflakes and snow on plants. Sublimation is the conversion of solid into vapor. The snow sublimation heat flux is the supply of latent heat which is causing evaporation of snow to water vapor. In accordance with common usage in geophysical disciplines, \"flux\" implies per unit area, called \"flux density\" in physics."@en;
        skos:notation          "SDN:P07::CFSN0221";
        skos:note              "accepted"@en;
        skos:prefLabel         "surface_snow_sublimation_heat_flux"@en;
        skos:related           <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P06/current/UFAA/> .

<http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/CFSN0500/>
        rdf:type               skos:Concept;
        dce:identifier         "SDN:P07::CFSN0500";
        dc:date                "2006-09-26 18:12:43.0";
        dc:identifier          "SDN:P07::CFSN0500";
        pav:authoredOn         "2006-09-26 18:12:43.0";
        pav:hasCurrentVersion  <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/CFSN0500/1/>;
        pav:version            "1";
        void:inDataset         <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/.well-known/void>;
        owl:deprecated         "false";
        owl:sameAs             <http://mmisw.org/ont/cf/parameter/net_upward_shortwave_flux_in_air_assuming_clear_sky> , <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/standard_name/net_upward_shortwave_flux_in_air_assuming_clear_sky/>;
        owl:versionInfo        "1";
        skos:altLabel          "";
        skos:broader           <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P64/current/G210/> , <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P04/current/G210/> , <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P64/current/G207/> , <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P64/current/G214/> , <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P04/current/G214/> , <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P02/current/CSLR/> , <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P04/current/G207/>;
        skos:definition        "A phrase assuming_condition indicates that the named quantity is the value which would obtain if all aspects of the system were unaltered except for the assumption of the circumstances specified by the condition. 'shortwave' means shortwave radiation. 'Upward' indicates a vector component which is positive when directed upward (negative downward). Net upward radiation is the difference between radiation from below (upwelling) and radiation from above (downwelling). In accordance with common usage in geophysical disciplines, 'flux' implies per unit area, called 'flux density' in physics."@en;
        skos:notation          "SDN:P07::CFSN0500";
        skos:note              "accepted"@en;
        skos:prefLabel         "net_upward_shortwave_flux_in_air_assuming_clear_sky"@en;
        skos:related           <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P06/current/UFAA/> .

<http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/2WTY2PMW/>
        rdf:type               skos:Concept;
        dce:identifier         "SDN:P07::2WTY2PMW";
        dc:date                "2018-02-12 16:53:18.0";
        dc:identifier          "SDN:P07::2WTY2PMW";
        pav:authoredOn         "2018-02-12 16:53:18.0";
        pav:hasCurrentVersion  <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/2WTY2PMW/1/>;
        pav:version            "1";
        void:inDataset         <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/.well-known/void>;
        owl:deprecated         "false";
        owl:sameAs             <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/standard_name/integral_wrt_time_of_radioactivity_concentration_of_223Rn_in_air/> , <http://mmisw.org/ont/cf/parameter/integral_wrt_time_of_radioactivity_concentration_of_223Rn_in_air>;
        owl:versionInfo        "1";
        skos:altLabel          "";
        skos:definition        "The phrase \"integral_wrt_X_of_Y\" means int Y dX. The data variable should have an axis for X specifying the limits of the integral as bounds. The phrase \"wrt\" means \"with respect to\". \"Radioactivity\" means the number of radioactive decays of a material per second. \"Radioactivity concentration\" means radioactivity per unit volume of the medium. \"Rn\" means the element \"radon\" and \"223Rn\" is the isotope \"radon-223\" with a half-life of 2.98e-02 days."@en;
        skos:notation          "SDN:P07::2WTY2PMW";
        skos:note              "accepted"@en;
        skos:prefLabel         "integral_wrt_time_of_radioactivity_concentration_of_223Rn_in_air"@en;
        skos:related           <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P06/current/BSM3/> .

<http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/I8R1394U/>
        rdf:type               skos:Concept;
        dce:identifier         "SDN:P07::I8R1394U";
        dc:date                "2018-02-12 16:53:19.0";
        dc:identifier          "SDN:P07::I8R1394U";
        dc:isReplacedBy        <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/3MUBQ4IM/>;
        pav:authoredOn         "2018-02-12 16:53:19.0";
        pav:hasCurrentVersion  <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/I8R1394U/2/>;
        pav:hasVersion         <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/I8R1394U/1/>;
        pav:version            "2";
        void:inDataset         <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/.well-known/void>;
        owl:deprecated         "true";
        owl:sameAs             <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/standard_name/tendency_of_ocean_eddy_kinetic_energy_content_due_to_parameterized_eddy_advection/> , <http://mmisw.org/ont/cf/parameter/tendency_of_ocean_eddy_kinetic_energy_content_due_to_parameterized_eddy_advection>;
        owl:versionInfo        "2";
        skos:altLabel          "";
        skos:definition        "\"tendency_of_X\" means derivative of X with respect to time. \"Content\" indicates a quantity per unit area. The \"ocean content\" of a quantity refers to the vertical integral from the surface to the bottom of the ocean. The specification of a physical process by the phrase \"due_to_\" process means that the quantity named is a single term in a sum of terms which together compose the general quantity named by omitting the phrase. Parameterized eddy advection in an ocean model means the part due to a scheme representing parameterized eddy-induced advective effects not included in the resolved model velocity field. Parameterized eddy advection can be represented on various spatial scales and there are standard names for parameterized_mesoscale_eddy_advection and parameterized_submesoscale_eddy_advection which both contribute to the total parameterized eddy advection."@en;
        skos:notation          "SDN:P07::I8R1394U";
        skos:note              "deprecated"@en;
        skos:prefLabel         "tendency_of_ocean_eddy_kinetic_energy_content_due_to_parameterized_eddy_advection"@en;
        skos:related           <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P06/current/UFAA/> .

