<http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/CF14N80/>
        rdf:type               skos:Concept;
        dce:identifier         "SDN:P07::CF14N80";
        dc:date                "2018-12-17 17:17:12.0";
        dc:identifier          "SDN:P07::CF14N80";
        pav:authoredOn         "2018-12-17 17:17:12.0";
        pav:hasCurrentVersion  <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/CF14N80/2/>;
        pav:hasVersion         <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/CF14N80/1/>;
        pav:version            "2";
        void:inDataset         <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/.well-known/void>;
        owl:deprecated         "false";
        owl:sameAs             <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/standard_name/tendency_of_mole_concentration_of_aragonite_expressed_as_carbon_in_sea_water_due_to_biological_production/> , <http://mmisw.org/ont/cf/parameter/tendency_of_mole_concentration_of_aragonite_expressed_as_carbon_in_sea_water_due_to_biological_production>;
        owl:versionInfo        "2";
        skos:altLabel          "";
        skos:broader           <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P02/current/CING/>;
        skos:definition        "The phrase \"tendency_of_X\" means derivative of X with respect to time. Mole concentration means number of moles per unit volume, also called \"molarity\", and is used in the construction mole_concentration_of_X_in_Y, where X is a material constituent of Y. A chemical or biological species denoted by X may be described by a single term such as \"nitrogen\" or a phrase such as \"nox_expressed_as_nitrogen\". The phrase \"expressed_as\" is used in the construction A_expressed_as_B, where B is a chemical constituent of A. It means that the quantity indicated by the standard name is calculated solely with respect to the B contained in A, neglecting all other chemical constituents of A. The specification of a physical process by the phrase \"due_to_\" process means that the quantity named is a single term in a sum of terms which together compose the general quantity named by omitting the phrase. Aragonite is a mineral that is a polymorph of calcium carbonate. The chemical formula of aragonite is CaCO3. Standard names also exist for calcite, another polymorph of calcium carbonate."@en;
        skos:notation          "SDN:P07::CF14N80";
        skos:note              "accepted"@en;
        skos:prefLabel         "tendency_of_mole_concentration_of_aragonite_expressed_as_carbon_in_sea_water_due_to_biological_production"@en;
        skos:related           <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P06/current/MM3S/> .

<http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/26XF5PHE/>
        rdf:type               skos:Concept;
        dce:identifier         "SDN:P07::26XF5PHE";
        dc:date                "2026-03-16 11:27:13.0";
        dc:identifier          "SDN:P07::26XF5PHE";
        pav:authoredOn         "2026-03-16 11:27:13.0";
        pav:hasCurrentVersion  <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/26XF5PHE/1/>;
        pav:version            "1";
        void:inDataset         <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/.well-known/void>;
        owl:deprecated         "false";
        owl:sameAs             <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/standard_name/sinking_mole_flux_at_sea_floor_of_particulate_organic_matter_expressed_as_carbon_in_sea_water/>;
        owl:versionInfo        "1";
        skos:altLabel          "";
        skos:definition        "The phrase \"expressed_as\" is used in the construction \"A_expressed_as_B\", where B is a chemical constituent of A. It means that the quantity indicated by the standard name is calculated solely with respect to the B contained in A, neglecting all other chemical constituents of A. In accordance with common usage in geophysical disciplines, \"flux\" implies per unit area, called \"flux density\" in physics. \"Sinking\" is the gravitational settling of particulate matter suspended in a liquid. A sinking flux is positive downwards and is calculated relative to the movement of the surrounding fluid. Particulate organic matter (also called POM) means organic matter that does not pass through a reference filter pore size. \"Organic matter\" means material which is derived from living organisms or from their biological activities. Organic matter consists of carbon-based compounds."@en;
        skos:notation          "SDN:P07::26XF5PHE";
        skos:note              "accepted"@en;
        skos:prefLabel         "sinking_mole_flux_at_sea_floor_of_particulate_organic_matter_expressed_as_carbon_in_sea_water"@en;
        skos:related           <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P06/current/M2MS/> .

<http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/CFSN0616/>
        rdf:type               skos:Concept;
        dce:identifier         "SDN:P07::CFSN0616";
        dc:date                "2017-07-24 14:36:59.0";
        dc:identifier          "SDN:P07::CFSN0616";
        dc:replaces            <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/CFSNA009/> , <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/CFSNA008/>;
        pav:authoredOn         "2017-07-24 14:36:59.0";
        pav:hasCurrentVersion  <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/CFSN0616/2/>;
        pav:hasVersion         <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/CFSN0616/1/>;
        pav:version            "2";
        void:inDataset         <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/.well-known/void>;
        owl:deprecated         "false";
        owl:sameAs             <http://mmisw.org/ont/cf/parameter/lagrangian_tendency_of_air_pressure> , <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/standard_name/lagrangian_tendency_of_air_pressure/> , <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/CFSNA008/> , <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/CFSNA009/>;
        owl:versionInfo        "2";
        skos:altLabel          "";
        skos:broader           <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P02/current/CAPH/> , <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P64/current/G192/> , <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P04/current/G192/>;
        skos:definition        "\"tendency_of_X\" means derivative of X with respect to time. The Lagrangian tendency of a quantity is its rate of change following the motion of the fluid, also called the \"material derivative\" or \"convective derivative\". The Lagrangian tendency of air pressure, often called \"omega\", plays the role of the upward component of air velocity when air pressure is being used as the vertical coordinate. If the vertical air velocity is upwards, it is negative when expressed as a tendency of air pressure; downwards is positive. Air pressure is the force per unit area which would be exerted when the moving gas molecules of which the air is composed strike a theoretical surface of any orientation."@en;
        skos:notation          "SDN:P07::CFSN0616";
        skos:note              "accepted"@en;
        skos:prefLabel         "lagrangian_tendency_of_air_pressure"@en;
        skos:related           <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P06/current/PAPS/> .

