<http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/CF12N496/>
        rdf:type               skos:Concept;
        dce:identifier         "SDN:P07::CF12N496";
        dc:date                "2015-01-07 16:23:38.0";
        dc:identifier          "SDN:P07::CF12N496";
        pav:authoredOn         "2015-01-07 16:23:38.0";
        pav:hasCurrentVersion  <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/CF12N496/3/>;
        pav:hasVersion         <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/CF12N496/1/> , <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/CF12N496/2/>;
        pav:version            "3";
        void:inDataset         <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/.well-known/void>;
        owl:deprecated         "false";
        owl:sameAs             <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/standard_name/mole_concentration_of_nmvoc_expressed_as_carbon_in_air/> , <http://mmisw.org/ont/cf/parameter/mole_concentration_of_nmvoc_expressed_as_carbon_in_air>;
        owl:versionInfo        "3";
        skos:altLabel          "";
        skos:broader           <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P02/current/OCAA/> , <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P04/current/G141/>;
        skos:definition        "Mole concentration means number of moles per unit volume, also called \"molarity\", and is used in the construction mole_concentration_of_X_in_Y, where X is a material constituent of Y.  A chemical species denoted by X may be described by a single term such as \"nitrogen\" or a phrase such as \"nox_expressed_as_nitrogen\". The phrase \"expressed_as\" is used in the construction A_expressed_as_B, where B is a chemical constituent of A. It means that the quantity indicated by the standard name is calculated solely with respect to the B contained in A, neglecting all other chemical constituents of A. \"nmvoc\" means non methane volatile organic compounds; \"nmvoc\" is the term used in standard names to describe the group of chemical species having this classification that are represented within a given model. The list of individual species that are included in a quantity having a group chemical standard name can vary between models. Where possible, the data variable should be accompanied by a complete description of the species represented, for example, by using a comment attribute."@en;
        skos:notation          "SDN:P07::CF12N496";
        skos:note              "accepted"@en;
        skos:prefLabel         "mole_concentration_of_nmvoc_expressed_as_carbon_in_air"@en;
        skos:related           <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P06/current/MLM3/> .

<http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/82BHCZEX/>
        rdf:type               skos:Concept;
        dce:identifier         "SDN:P07::82BHCZEX";
        dc:date                "2018-02-12 16:53:18.0";
        dc:identifier          "SDN:P07::82BHCZEX";
        pav:authoredOn         "2018-02-12 16:53:18.0";
        pav:hasCurrentVersion  <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/82BHCZEX/1/>;
        pav:version            "1";
        void:inDataset         <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/.well-known/void>;
        owl:deprecated         "false";
        owl:sameAs             <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/standard_name/surface_radioactivity_content_of_134mI/> , <http://mmisw.org/ont/cf/parameter/surface_radioactivity_content_of_134mI>;
        owl:versionInfo        "1";
        skos:altLabel          "";
        skos:definition        "The surface called \"surface\" means the lower boundary of the atmosphere. \"Content\" indicates a quantity per unit area. \"Radioactivity\" means the number of radioactive decays of a material per second. \"I\" means the element \"iodine\" and \"134mI\" is the metastable state of the isotope \"iodine-134\" with a half-life of 2.50e-03 days."@en;
        skos:notation          "SDN:P07::82BHCZEX";
        skos:note              "accepted"@en;
        skos:prefLabel         "surface_radioactivity_content_of_134mI"@en;
        skos:related           <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P06/current/BQSM/> .

<http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/RU0W7DC0/>
        rdf:type               skos:Concept;
        dce:identifier         "SDN:P07::RU0W7DC0";
        dc:date                "2025-01-20 16:07:13.0";
        dc:identifier          "SDN:P07::RU0W7DC0";
        pav:authoredOn         "2025-01-20 16:07:13.0";
        pav:hasCurrentVersion  <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/RU0W7DC0/2/>;
        pav:hasVersion         <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/RU0W7DC0/1/>;
        pav:version            "2";
        void:inDataset         <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/.well-known/void>;
        owl:deprecated         "false";
        owl:sameAs             <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/standard_name/tendency_of_atmosphere_mole_concentration_of_molecular_hydrogen_due_to_chemical_destruction/>;
        owl:versionInfo        "2";
        skos:altLabel          "";
        skos:definition        "\"Mole concentration\" means number of moles per unit volume, also called \"molarity\", and is used in the construction \"mole_concentration_of_X_in_Y\", where X is a material constituent of Y. A chemical or biological species denoted by X may be described by a single term such as \"nitrogen\" or a phrase such as \"nox_expressed_as_nitrogen\". The phrase \"tendency_of_X\" means derivative of X with respect to time. The specification of a physical process by the phrase \"due_to_\" process means that the quantity named is a single term in a sum of terms which together compose the general quantity named by omitting the phrase. \"Chemical destruction\" means the result of all chemical reactions within the medium (here, atmosphere) that remove a certain amount of a particular species from the medium. The chemical formula for molecular hydrogen is H2."@en;
        skos:notation          "SDN:P07::RU0W7DC0";
        skos:note              "accepted"@en;
        skos:prefLabel         "tendency_of_atmosphere_mole_concentration_of_molecular_hydrogen_due_to_chemical_destruction"@en;
        skos:related           <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P06/current/MM3S/> .

<http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/CF12N3/>
        rdf:type               skos:Concept;
        dce:identifier         "SDN:P07::CF12N3";
        dc:date                "2009-07-06 15:41:29.0";
        dc:identifier          "SDN:P07::CF12N3";
        pav:authoredOn         "2009-07-06 15:41:29.0";
        pav:hasCurrentVersion  <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/CF12N3/1/>;
        pav:version            "1";
        void:inDataset         <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/.well-known/void>;
        owl:deprecated         "false";
        owl:sameAs             <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/standard_name/atmosphere_mass_content_of_alkanes/> , <http://mmisw.org/ont/cf/parameter/atmosphere_mass_content_of_alkanes>;
        owl:versionInfo        "1";
        skos:altLabel          "";
        skos:broader           <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P04/current/G140/> , <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P02/current/AALK/>;
        skos:definition        "\"Content\" indicates a quantity per unit area.  The \"atmosphere content\" of a quantity refers to the vertical integral from the surface to the top of the atmosphere. For the content between specified levels in the atmosphere, standard names including content_of_atmosphere_layer are used.  Alkanes are saturated hydrocarbons, i.e. they do not contain any chemical double bonds.  Alkanes contain only hydrogen and carbon combined in the general proportions C(n)H(2n+2); \"alkanes\" is the term used in standard names to describe the group of chemical species having this common structure that are represented within a given model. The list of individual species that are included in a quantity having a group chemical standard name can vary between models.  Where possible, the data variable should be accompanied by a complete description of the species represented, for example, by using a comment attribute. Standard names exist for some individual alkane species, e.g., methane and ethane."@en;
        skos:notation          "SDN:P07::CF12N3";
        skos:note              "accepted"@en;
        skos:prefLabel         "atmosphere_mass_content_of_alkanes"@en;
        skos:related           <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P06/current/KMP2/> .

<http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/CFSN0090/>
        rdf:type               skos:Concept;
        dce:identifier         "SDN:P07::CFSN0090";
        dc:date                "2024-09-04 12:51:22.0";
        dc:identifier          "SDN:P07::CFSN0090";
        pav:authoredOn         "2024-09-04 12:51:22.0";
        pav:hasCurrentVersion  <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/CFSN0090/3/>;
        pav:hasVersion         <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/CFSN0090/1/> , <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/CFSN0090/2/>;
        pav:version            "3";
        void:inDataset         <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/.well-known/void>;
        owl:deprecated         "false";
        owl:sameAs             <http://mmisw.org/ont/cf/parameter/vegetation_area_fraction> , <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/standard_name/vegetation_area_fraction/>;
        owl:versionInfo        "3";
        skos:altLabel          "";
        skos:broader           <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P02/current/HBEX/> , <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P02/current/LVEG/> , <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P04/current/G444/> , <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P64/current/G444/>;
        skos:definition        "\"Area fraction\" is the fraction of a grid cell's horizontal area that has some characteristic of interest. It is evaluated as the area of interest divided by the grid cell area, or if the cell_methods restricts the evaluation to some portion of that grid cell (e.g. \"where sea_ice\"), then it is the area of interest divided by the area of the identified portion. It may be expressed as a fraction, a percentage, or any other dimensionless representation of a fraction. \"Vegetation\" means any plants e.g. trees, shrubs, grass. The term \"plants\" refers to the kingdom of plants in the modern classification which excludes fungi. Plants are autotrophs i.e. \"producers\" of biomass using carbon obtained from carbon dioxide."@en;
        skos:notation          "SDN:P07::CFSN0090";
        skos:note              "accepted"@en;
        skos:prefLabel         "vegetation_area_fraction"@en;
        skos:related           <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P06/current/UUUU/> .

<http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/95FD7XQJ/>
        rdf:type               skos:Concept;
        dce:identifier         "SDN:P07::95FD7XQJ";
        dc:date                "2013-03-23 09:22:59.0";
        dc:identifier          "SDN:P07::95FD7XQJ";
        pav:authoredOn         "2013-03-23 09:22:59.0";
        pav:hasCurrentVersion  <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/95FD7XQJ/1/>;
        pav:version            "1";
        void:inDataset         <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/.well-known/void>;
        owl:deprecated         "false";
        owl:sameAs             <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/standard_name/stratosphere_mole_content_of_nitrogen_dioxide/> , <http://mmisw.org/ont/cf/parameter/stratosphere_mole_content_of_nitrogen_dioxide>;
        owl:versionInfo        "1";
        skos:altLabel          "";
        skos:broader           <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P02/current/AAMN/>;
        skos:definition        "\"Content\" indicates a quantity per unit area. The \"stratosphere content\" of a quantity refers to the vertical integral from the tropopause to the stratopause. For the content between specified levels in the atmosphere, standard names including content_of_atmosphere_layer are used. The chemical formula for nitrogen_dioxide is NO2."@en;
        skos:notation          "SDN:P07::95FD7XQJ";
        skos:note              "accepted"@en;
        skos:prefLabel         "stratosphere_mole_content_of_nitrogen_dioxide"@en;
        skos:related           <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P06/current/MLM2/> .

<http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/CF12N739/>
        rdf:type               skos:Concept;
        dce:identifier         "SDN:P07::CF12N739";
        dc:date                "2009-07-06 15:41:29.0";
        dc:identifier          "SDN:P07::CF12N739";
        pav:authoredOn         "2009-07-06 15:41:29.0";
        pav:hasCurrentVersion  <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/CF12N739/1/>;
        pav:version            "1";
        void:inDataset         <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/.well-known/void>;
        owl:deprecated         "false";
        owl:sameAs             <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/standard_name/tendency_of_atmosphere_moles_of_propene/> , <http://mmisw.org/ont/cf/parameter/tendency_of_atmosphere_moles_of_propene>;
        owl:versionInfo        "1";
        skos:altLabel          "";
        skos:broader           <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P04/current/G140/> , <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P02/current/AEYN/>;
        skos:definition        "\"tendency_of_X\" means derivative of X with respect to time.  The construction \"atmosphere_moles_of_X\" means the total number of moles of X in the entire atmosphere, i.e. summed over the atmospheric column and over the entire globe. The chemical formula for propene is C3H6. Propene is a member of the group of hydrocarbons known as alkenes.  There are standard names for the alkene group as well as for some of the individual species."@en;
        skos:notation          "SDN:P07::CF12N739";
        skos:note              "accepted"@en;
        skos:prefLabel         "tendency_of_atmosphere_moles_of_propene"@en;
        skos:related           <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P06/current/MLPS/> .

<http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/D4AEX5R2/>
        rdf:type               skos:Concept;
        dce:identifier         "SDN:P07::D4AEX5R2";
        dc:date                "2018-02-12 16:53:18.0";
        dc:identifier          "SDN:P07::D4AEX5R2";
        pav:authoredOn         "2018-02-12 16:53:18.0";
        pav:hasCurrentVersion  <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/D4AEX5R2/1/>;
        pav:version            "1";
        void:inDataset         <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/.well-known/void>;
        owl:deprecated         "false";
        owl:sameAs             <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/standard_name/surface_radioactivity_content_of_119In/> , <http://mmisw.org/ont/cf/parameter/surface_radioactivity_content_of_119In>;
        owl:versionInfo        "1";
        skos:altLabel          "";
        skos:definition        "The surface called \"surface\" means the lower boundary of the atmosphere. \"Content\" indicates a quantity per unit area. \"Radioactivity\" means the number of radioactive decays of a material per second. \"In\" means the element \"indium\" and \"119In\" is the isotope \"indium-119\" with a half-life of 1.74e-03 days."@en;
        skos:notation          "SDN:P07::D4AEX5R2";
        skos:note              "accepted"@en;
        skos:prefLabel         "surface_radioactivity_content_of_119In"@en;
