<http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/S036R4PA/>
        rdf:type               skos:Concept;
        dce:identifier         "SDN:P07::S036R4PA";
        dc:date                "2021-01-18 16:23:44.0";
        dc:identifier          "SDN:P07::S036R4PA";
        pav:authoredOn         "2021-01-18 16:23:44.0";
        pav:hasCurrentVersion  <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/S036R4PA/2/>;
        pav:hasVersion         <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/S036R4PA/1/>;
        pav:version            "2";
        void:inDataset         <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/.well-known/void>;
        owl:deprecated         "false";
        owl:sameAs             <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/standard_name/liquid_water_mass_flux_into_soil_due_to_surface_snow_melt/> , <http://mmisw.org/ont/cf/parameter/liquid_water_mass_flux_into_soil_due_to_surface_snow_melt>;
        owl:versionInfo        "2";
        skos:altLabel          "";
        skos:definition        "In accordance with common usage in geophysical disciplines, \"flux\" implies per unit area, called \"flux density\" in physics. Surface snow refers to the snow on the solid ground or on surface ice cover, but excludes, for example, falling snowflakes and snow on plants. The specification of a physical process by the phrase \"due_to_\" process means that the quantity named is a single term in a sum of terms which together compose the general quantity named by omitting the phrase."@en;
        skos:notation          "SDN:P07::S036R4PA";
        skos:note              "accepted"@en;
        skos:prefLabel         "liquid_water_mass_flux_into_soil_due_to_surface_snow_melt"@en;
        skos:related           <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P06/current/KSP2/> .

<http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/RFG9S0U3/>
        rdf:type               skos:Concept;
        dce:identifier         "SDN:P07::RFG9S0U3";
        dc:date                "2018-02-12 16:53:18.0";
        dc:identifier          "SDN:P07::RFG9S0U3";
        pav:authoredOn         "2018-02-12 16:53:18.0";
        pav:hasCurrentVersion  <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/RFG9S0U3/1/>;
        pav:version            "1";
        void:inDataset         <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/.well-known/void>;
        owl:deprecated         "false";
        owl:sameAs             <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/standard_name/surface_radioactivity_content_of_129Sb/> , <http://mmisw.org/ont/cf/parameter/surface_radioactivity_content_of_129Sb>;
        owl:versionInfo        "1";
        skos:altLabel          "";
        skos:definition        "The surface called \"surface\" means the lower boundary of the atmosphere. \"Content\" indicates a quantity per unit area. \"Radioactivity\" means the number of radioactive decays of a material per second. \"Sb\" means the element \"antimony\" and \"129Sb\" is the isotope \"antimony-129\" with a half-life of 1.81e-01 days."@en;
        skos:notation          "SDN:P07::RFG9S0U3";
        skos:note              "accepted"@en;
        skos:prefLabel         "surface_radioactivity_content_of_129Sb"@en;
        skos:related           <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P06/current/BQSM/> .

<http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/KKU5IUR9/>
        rdf:type               skos:Concept;
        dce:identifier         "SDN:P07::KKU5IUR9";
        dc:date                "2018-05-29 16:57:01.0";
        dc:identifier          "SDN:P07::KKU5IUR9";
        pav:authoredOn         "2018-05-29 16:57:01.0";
        pav:hasCurrentVersion  <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/KKU5IUR9/1/>;
        pav:version            "1";
        void:inDataset         <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/.well-known/void>;
        owl:deprecated         "false";
        owl:sameAs             <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/standard_name/atmosphere_transformed_eulerian_mean_meridional_overturning_mass_streamfunction/> , <http://mmisw.org/ont/cf/parameter/atmosphere_transformed_eulerian_mean_meridional_overturning_mass_streamfunction>;
        owl:versionInfo        "1";
        skos:altLabel          "";
        skos:definition        "The \"meridional mass streamfunction\" is a streamfunction of the zonally averaged mass transport in the meridional plane. The \"Transformed Eulerian Mean\" refers to a formulation of the mean equations which incorporates some eddy terms into the definition of the mean, described in Andrews et al (1987): Middle Atmospheric Dynamics. Academic Press."@en;
        skos:notation          "SDN:P07::KKU5IUR9";
        skos:note              "accepted"@en;
        skos:prefLabel         "atmosphere_transformed_eulerian_mean_meridional_overturning_mass_streamfunction"@en;
        skos:related           <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P06/current/KGPS/> .

<http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/CF12N564/>
        rdf:type               skos:Concept;
        dce:identifier         "SDN:P07::CF12N564";
        dc:date                "2009-07-06 15:41:29.0";
        dc:identifier          "SDN:P07::CF12N564";
        pav:authoredOn         "2009-07-06 15:41:29.0";
        pav:hasCurrentVersion  <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/CF12N564/1/>;
        pav:version            "1";
        void:inDataset         <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/.well-known/void>;
        owl:deprecated         "false";
        owl:sameAs             <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/standard_name/ocean_tracer_epineutral_laplacian_diffusivity/> , <http://mmisw.org/ont/cf/parameter/ocean_tracer_epineutral_laplacian_diffusivity>;
        owl:versionInfo        "1";
        skos:altLabel          "";
        skos:definition        "Diffusivity is also sometimes known as the coefficient of diffusion. Diffusion occurs as a result of a gradient in the spatial distribution of mass concentration, temperature or momentum.  The diffusivity may be very different in the vertical and horizontal directions. \"epineutral diffusivity\" means a lateral diffusivity along a either a neutral or isopycnal density surface due to motion which is not resolved on the grid scale of an ocean model. The type of density surface is dependent on the model formulation. \"laplacian diffusivity\" means diffusivity for use with a Laplacian diffusion operator."@en;
        skos:notation          "SDN:P07::CF12N564";
        skos:note              "accepted"@en;
        skos:prefLabel         "ocean_tracer_epineutral_laplacian_diffusivity"@en;
        skos:related           <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P06/current/SQMS/> , <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P04/current/G915/> , <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P64/current/G915/> .

<http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/1UZBN991/>
        rdf:type               skos:Concept;
        dce:identifier         "SDN:P07::1UZBN991";
        dc:date                "2018-02-12 16:53:18.0";
