<http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/D32N6YZZ/>
        rdf:type               skos:Concept;
        dce:identifier         "SDN:P07::D32N6YZZ";
        dc:date                "2018-04-16 15:20:49.0";
        dc:identifier          "SDN:P07::D32N6YZZ";
        pav:authoredOn         "2018-04-16 15:20:49.0";
        pav:hasCurrentVersion  <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/D32N6YZZ/1/>;
        pav:version            "1";
        void:inDataset         <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/.well-known/void>;
        owl:deprecated         "false";
        owl:sameAs             <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/standard_name/surface_upward_mass_flux_of_nox_expressed_as_nitrogen_out_of_vegetation_and_litter_and_soil/> , <http://mmisw.org/ont/cf/parameter/surface_upward_mass_flux_of_nox_expressed_as_nitrogen_out_of_vegetation_and_litter_and_soil>;
        owl:versionInfo        "1";
        skos:altLabel          "";
        skos:definition        "The surface called \"surface\" means the lower boundary of the atmosphere. \"Upward\" indicates a vector component which is positive when directed upward (negative downward). In accordance with common usage in geophysical disciplines, \"flux\" implies per unit area, called \"flux density\" in physics. The phrase \"expressed_as\" is used in the construction A_expressed_as_B, where B is a chemical constituent of A. It means that the quantity indicated by the standard name is calculated solely with respect to the B contained in A, neglecting all other chemical constituents of A. \"Nox\" means a combination of two radical species containing nitrogen and oxygen NO+NO2. \"Vegetation\" means any living plants e.g. trees, shrubs, grass. \"Litter\" is dead plant material in or above the soil."@en;
        skos:notation          "SDN:P07::D32N6YZZ";
        skos:note              "accepted"@en;
        skos:prefLabel         "surface_upward_mass_flux_of_nox_expressed_as_nitrogen_out_of_vegetation_and_litter_and_soil"@en;
        skos:related           <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P06/current/KSP2/> .

<http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/CF12N436/>
        rdf:type               skos:Concept;
        dce:identifier         "SDN:P07::CF12N436";
        dc:date                "2009-07-06 15:41:29.0";
        dc:identifier          "SDN:P07::CF12N436";
        pav:authoredOn         "2009-07-06 15:41:29.0";
        pav:hasCurrentVersion  <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/CF12N436/1/>;
        pav:version            "1";
        void:inDataset         <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/.well-known/void>;
        owl:deprecated         "false";
        owl:sameAs             <http://mmisw.org/ont/cf/parameter/mole_concentration_of_carbon_dioxide_in_air> , <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/standard_name/mole_concentration_of_carbon_dioxide_in_air/>;
        owl:versionInfo        "1";
        skos:altLabel          "";
        skos:broader           <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P02/current/ACO2/> , <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P04/current/G132/> , <http://rdfdata.eionet.europa.eu/airquality/components/71>;
        skos:definition        "Mole concentration means number of moles per unit volume, also called \"molarity\", and is used in the construction mole_concentration_of_X_in_Y, where X is a material constituent of Y.  A chemical species denoted by X may be described by a single term such as 'nitrogen' or a phrase such as 'nox_expressed_as_nitrogen'. The chemical formula for carbon dioxide is CO2."@en;
        skos:notation          "SDN:P07::CF12N436";
        skos:note              "accepted"@en;
        skos:prefLabel         "mole_concentration_of_carbon_dioxide_in_air"@en;
        skos:related           <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P06/current/MLM3/> .

<http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/CF12N536/>
        rdf:type               skos:Concept;
        dce:identifier         "SDN:P07::CF12N536";
        dc:date                "2019-02-04 14:52:10.0";
        dc:identifier          "SDN:P07::CF12N536";
        pav:authoredOn         "2019-02-04 14:52:10.0";
        pav:hasCurrentVersion  <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/CF12N536/2/>;
        pav:hasVersion         <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/CF12N536/1/>;
        pav:version            "2";
        void:inDataset         <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/.well-known/void>;
        owl:deprecated         "false";
        owl:sameAs             <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/standard_name/mole_fraction_of_radon_in_air/> , <http://mmisw.org/ont/cf/parameter/mole_fraction_of_radon_in_air>;
        owl:versionInfo        "2";
        skos:altLabel          "";
        skos:definition        "\"Mole fraction\" is used in the construction \"mole_fraction_of_X_in_Y\", where X is a material constituent of Y. A chemical or biological species denoted by X may be described by a single term such as \"nitrogen\" or a phrase such as \"nox_expressed_as_nitrogen\". The chemical symbol for radon is Rn."@en;
        skos:notation          "SDN:P07::CF12N536";
        skos:note              "accepted"@en;
        skos:prefLabel         "mole_fraction_of_radon_in_air"@en;
        skos:related           <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P06/current/UUUU/> .

<http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/8Z4A6V06/>
        rdf:type               skos:Concept;
        dce:identifier         "SDN:P07::8Z4A6V06";
        dc:date                "2024-05-20 20:39:49.0";
        dc:identifier          "SDN:P07::8Z4A6V06";
        pav:authoredOn         "2024-05-20 20:39:49.0";
        pav:hasCurrentVersion  <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/8Z4A6V06/2/>;
        pav:hasVersion         <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/8Z4A6V06/1/>;
        pav:version            "2";
        void:inDataset         <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/.well-known/void>;
        owl:deprecated         "false";
        owl:sameAs             <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/standard_name/air_equivalent_temperature/> , <http://mmisw.org/ont/cf/parameter/air_equivalent_temperature> , <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/CFSN0659/>;
        owl:versionInfo        "2";
        skos:altLabel          "";
        skos:definition        "The equivalent temperature is the temperature that an air parcel would have if all water vapor were condensed at contstant pressure and the enthalpy released from the vapor used to heat the air. Reference: AMS Glossary http://glossary.ametsoc.org/wiki/Equivalent_temperature. It is the isobaric equivalent temperature and not the adiabatic equivalent temperature, also known as pseudoequivalent temperature, which has the standard name air_pseudo_equivalent_temperature. It is strongly recommended that a variable with this standard name should have a units_metadata attribute, with one of the values \"on-scale\" or \"difference\", whichever is appropriate for the data, because it is essential to know whether the temperature is on-scale (meaning relative to the origin of the scale indicated by the units) or refers to temperature differences (implying that the origin of the temperature scale is irrevelant), in order to convert the units correctly (cf. https://cfconventions.org/cf-conventions/cf-conventions.html#temperature-units)."@en;
        skos:notation          "SDN:P07::8Z4A6V06";
        skos:note              "accepted"@en;
        skos:prefLabel         "air_equivalent_temperature"@en;
        skos:related           <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P06/current/UPKA/> .

<http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/CFSN0659/>
        rdf:type               skos:Concept;
        dce:identifier         "SDN:P07::CFSN0659";
        dc:date                "2020-03-09 15:56:08.0";
