        pav:version            "2";
        void:inDataset         <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/.well-known/void>;
        owl:deprecated         "false";
        owl:sameAs             <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/standard_name/tendency_of_atmosphere_mass_content_of_nitrate_dry_aerosol_particles_due_to_dry_deposition/> , <http://mmisw.org/ont/cf/parameter/tendency_of_atmosphere_mass_content_of_nitrate_dry_aerosol_particles_due_to_dry_deposition>;
        owl:versionInfo        "2";
        skos:altLabel          "";
        skos:definition        "\"Content\" indicates a quantity per unit area. The \"atmosphere content\" of a quantity refers to the vertical integral from the surface to the top of the atmosphere. For the content between specified levels in the atmosphere, standard names including content_of_atmosphere_layer are used. The mass is the total mass of the particles. \"Aerosol\" means the system of suspended liquid or solid particles in air (except cloud droplets) and their carrier gas, the air itself. Aerosol particles take up ambient water (a process known as hygroscopic growth) depending on the relative humidity and the composition of the particles. \"Dry aerosol particles\" means aerosol particles without any water uptake. The specification of a physical process by the phrase due_to_process means that the quantity named is a  single term in a sum of terms which together compose the general quantity named by omitting the phrase. \"Dry deposition\" is the sum of turbulent deposition and gravitational settling.  \"tendency_of_X\" means derivative of X with respect to time. The chemical formula for the nitrate anion is NO3-."@en;
        skos:notation          "SDN:P07::RV4GJ49C";
        skos:note              "accepted"@en;
        skos:prefLabel         "tendency_of_atmosphere_mass_content_of_nitrate_dry_aerosol_particles_due_to_dry_deposition"@en;
        skos:related           <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P06/current/KSP2/> .

<http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/7CQEXEJE/>
        rdf:type               skos:Concept;
        dce:identifier         "SDN:P07::7CQEXEJE";
        dc:date                "2018-02-12 16:53:18.0";
        dc:identifier          "SDN:P07::7CQEXEJE";
        pav:authoredOn         "2018-02-12 16:53:18.0";
        pav:hasCurrentVersion  <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/7CQEXEJE/1/>;
        pav:version            "1";
        void:inDataset         <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/.well-known/void>;
        owl:deprecated         "false";
        owl:sameAs             <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/standard_name/surface_radioactivity_content_of_116In/> , <http://mmisw.org/ont/cf/parameter/surface_radioactivity_content_of_116In>;
        owl:versionInfo        "1";
        skos:altLabel          "";
        skos:definition        "The surface called \"surface\" means the lower boundary of the atmosphere. \"Content\" indicates a quantity per unit area. \"Radioactivity\" means the number of radioactive decays of a material per second. \"In\" means the element \"indium\" and \"116In\" is the isotope \"indium-116\" with a half-life of 1.64e-04 days."@en;
        skos:notation          "SDN:P07::7CQEXEJE";
        skos:note              "accepted"@en;
        skos:prefLabel         "surface_radioactivity_content_of_116In"@en;
        skos:related           <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P06/current/BQSM/> .

<http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/2XJXW5QA/>
        rdf:type               skos:Concept;
        dce:identifier         "SDN:P07::2XJXW5QA";
        dc:date                "2015-01-07 16:23:38.0";
        dc:identifier          "SDN:P07::2XJXW5QA";
        dc:replaces            <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/CFV16A32/>;
        pav:authoredOn         "2015-01-07 16:23:38.0";
        pav:hasCurrentVersion  <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/2XJXW5QA/2/>;
        pav:hasVersion         <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/2XJXW5QA/1/>;
        pav:version            "2";
        void:inDataset         <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/.well-known/void>;
        owl:deprecated         "false";
        owl:sameAs             <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/standard_name/number_concentration_of_nucleation_mode_ambient_aerosol_particles_in_air/> , <http://mmisw.org/ont/cf/parameter/number_concentration_of_nucleation_mode_ambient_aerosol_particles_in_air>;
        owl:versionInfo        "2";
        skos:altLabel          "";
        skos:definition        "\"Number concentration\" means the number of particles or other specified objects per unit volume. \"Ambient_aerosol\" means that the aerosol is measured or modelled at the ambient state of pressure, temperature and relative humidity that exists in its immediate environment. \"Ambient aerosol particles\" are aerosol particles that have taken up ambient water through hygroscopic growth. The extent of hygroscopic growth depends on the relative humidity and the composition of the particles. Nucleation mode aerosol particles have a diameter of less than 3 nanometers."@en;
        skos:notation          "SDN:P07::2XJXW5QA";
        skos:note              "accepted"@en;
        skos:prefLabel         "number_concentration_of_nucleation_mode_ambient_aerosol_particles_in_air"@en;
        skos:related           <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P06/current/PCUM/> .

<http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/CFV16A32/>
        rdf:type               skos:Concept;
        dce:identifier         "SDN:P07::CFV16A32";
        dc:date                "2015-01-07 15:23:08.0";
        dc:identifier          "SDN:P07::CFV16A32";
