        pav:hasVersion         <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/CFV16A32/1/>;
        pav:version            "2";
        void:inDataset         <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/.well-known/void>;
        owl:deprecated         "true";
        owl:sameAs             <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/standard_name/number_concentration_of_nucleation_mode_ambient_aerosol_in_air/> , <http://mmisw.org/ont/cf/parameter/number_concentration_of_nucleation_mode_ambient_aerosol_in_air>;
        owl:versionInfo        "2";
        skos:altLabel          "";
        skos:definition        "\"Number concentration\" means the number of particles or other specified objects per unit volume. \"Aerosol\" means the suspended liquid or solid particles in air (except cloud droplets).  \"Ambient aerosol\" is aerosol that has taken up ambient water through hygroscopic growth. The extent of hygroscopic growth depends on the relative humidity and the composition of the aerosol. Nucleation mode aerosol is aerosol having a diameter of less than 3 nanometers."@en;
        skos:notation          "SDN:P07::CFV16A32";
        skos:note              "deprecated"@en;
        skos:prefLabel         "number_concentration_of_nucleation_mode_ambient_aerosol_in_air"@en;
        skos:related           <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P06/current/PCUM/> .

<http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/7XIDLX9W/>
        rdf:type               skos:Concept;
        dce:identifier         "SDN:P07::7XIDLX9W";
        dc:date                "2018-05-29 16:57:01.0";
        dc:identifier          "SDN:P07::7XIDLX9W";
        pav:authoredOn         "2018-05-29 16:57:01.0";
        pav:hasCurrentVersion  <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/7XIDLX9W/1/>;
        pav:version            "1";
        void:inDataset         <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/.well-known/void>;
        owl:deprecated         "false";
        owl:sameAs             <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/standard_name/tendency_of_eastward_wind_due_to_advection_by_northward_transformed_eulerian_mean_air_velocity/> , <http://mmisw.org/ont/cf/parameter/tendency_of_eastward_wind_due_to_advection_by_northward_transformed_eulerian_mean_air_velocity>;
        owl:versionInfo        "1";
        skos:altLabel          "";
        skos:definition        "The phrase \"tendency_of_X\" means derivative of X with respect to time. \"Eastward\" indicates a vector component which is positive when directed eastward (negative westward). Wind is defined as a two-dimensional (horizontal) air velocity vector, with no vertical component. (Vertical motion in the atmosphere has the standard name \"upward_air_velocity\"). The specification of a physical process by the phrase \"due_to_\" process means that the quantity named is a single term in a sum of terms which together compose the general quantity named by omitting the phrase. The \"Transformed Eulerian Mean\" refers to a formulation of the mean equations which incorporates some eddy terms into the definition of the mean, described in Andrews et al (1987): Middle Atmospheric Dynamics. Academic Press."@en;
        skos:notation          "SDN:P07::7XIDLX9W";
        skos:note              "accepted"@en;
        skos:prefLabel         "tendency_of_eastward_wind_due_to_advection_by_northward_transformed_eulerian_mean_air_velocity"@en;
        skos:related           <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P06/current/MPS2/> .

<http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/FHMB0C0Z/>
        rdf:type               skos:Concept;
        dce:identifier         "SDN:P07::FHMB0C0Z";
        dc:date                "2018-04-16 15:20:52.0";
        dc:identifier          "SDN:P07::FHMB0C0Z";
        pav:authoredOn         "2018-04-16 15:20:52.0";
        pav:hasCurrentVersion  <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/FHMB0C0Z/2/>;
        pav:hasVersion         <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/FHMB0C0Z/1/>;
        pav:version            "2";
        void:inDataset         <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/.well-known/void>;
        owl:deprecated         "true";
        owl:sameAs             <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/19EPEI15/> , <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/standard_name/surface_downward_mass_flux_of_carbon13_dioxide_abiotic_analogue_expressed_as_carbon13/> , <http://mmisw.org/ont/cf/parameter/surface_downward_mass_flux_of_carbon13_dioxide_abiotic_analogue_expressed_as_carbon13>;
        owl:versionInfo        "2";
        skos:altLabel          "";
        skos:definition        "The surface called \"surface\" means the lower boundary of the atmosphere. \"Downward\" indicates a vector component which is positive when directed downward (negative upward). In accordance with common usage in geophysical disciplines, \"flux\" implies per unit area, called \"flux density\" in physics. In ocean biogeochemistry models, an \"abiotic analogue\" is used to simulate the effect on a modelled variable when biological effects on ocean carbon concentration and alkalinity are ignored. The phrase \"expressed_as\" is used in the construction A_expressed_as_B, where B is a chemical constituent of A. It means that the quantity indicated by the standard name is calculated solely with respect to the B contained in A, neglecting all other chemical constituents of A. Carbon13 is a stable isotope of carbon having six protons and seven neutrons."@en;
        skos:notation          "SDN:P07::FHMB0C0Z";
        skos:note              "deprecated"@en;
        skos:prefLabel         "surface_downward_mass_flux_of_carbon13_dioxide_abiotic_analogue_expressed_as_carbon13"@en;
        skos:related           <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P06/current/KSP2/> .

<http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/19EPEI15/>
        rdf:type               skos:Concept;
        dce:identifier         "SDN:P07::19EPEI15";
        dc:date                "2018-04-16 15:20:49.0";
        dc:identifier          "SDN:P07::19EPEI15";
        pav:authoredOn         "2018-04-16 15:20:49.0";
        pav:hasCurrentVersion  <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/19EPEI15/1/>;
        pav:version            "1";
        void:inDataset         <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/.well-known/void>;
        owl:deprecated         "false";
        owl:sameAs             <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/standard_name/surface_downward_mass_flux_of_13C_dioxide_abiotic_analogue_expressed_as_13C/> , <http://mmisw.org/ont/cf/parameter/surface_downward_mass_flux_of_13C_dioxide_abiotic_analogue_expressed_as_13C> , <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/FHMB0C0Z/>;
        owl:versionInfo        "1";
        skos:altLabel          "";
        skos:definition        "The surface called \"surface\" means the lower boundary of the atmosphere. \"Downward\" indicates a vector component which is positive when directed downward (negative upward). In accordance with common usage in geophysical disciplines, \"flux\" implies per unit area, called \"flux density\" in physics. In ocean biogeochemistry models, an \"abiotic analogue\" is used to simulate the effect on a modelled variable when biological effects on ocean carbon concentration and alkalinity are ignored. The phrase \"expressed_as\" is used in the construction A_expressed_as_B, where B is a chemical constituent of A. It means that the quantity indicated by the standard name is calculated solely with respect to the B contained in A, neglecting all other chemical constituents of A. \"C\" means the element carbon and \"13C\" is the stable isotope \"carbon-13\", having six protons and seven neutrons."@en;
        skos:notation          "SDN:P07::19EPEI15";
        skos:note              "accepted"@en;
        skos:prefLabel         "surface_downward_mass_flux_of_13C_dioxide_abiotic_analogue_expressed_as_13C"@en;
        skos:related           <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P06/current/KSP2/> .

<http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/CFV8N16/>
        rdf:type               skos:Concept;
