<http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/CF12N336/>
        rdf:type               skos:Concept;
        dce:identifier         "SDN:P07::CF12N336";
        dc:date                "2009-07-06 15:41:29.0";
        dc:identifier          "SDN:P07::CF12N336";
        pav:authoredOn         "2009-07-06 15:41:29.0";
        pav:hasCurrentVersion  <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/CF12N336/1/>;
        pav:version            "1";
        void:inDataset         <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/.well-known/void>;
        owl:deprecated         "false";
        owl:sameAs             <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/standard_name/mass_fraction_of_atomic_chlorine_in_air/> , <http://mmisw.org/ont/cf/parameter/mass_fraction_of_atomic_chlorine_in_air>;
        owl:versionInfo        "1";
        skos:altLabel          "";
        skos:broader           <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P02/current/HLAT/> , <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P04/current/H321/>;
        skos:definition        "Mass fraction is used in the construction mass_fraction_of_X_in_Y, where X is a material constituent of Y. It means the ratio of the mass of X to the mass of Y (including X).  A chemical species denoted by X may be described by a single term such as 'nitrogen' or a phrase such as 'nox_expressed_as_nitrogen'. The chemical symbol for atomic chlorine is Cl."@en;
        skos:notation          "SDN:P07::CF12N336";
        skos:note              "accepted"@en;
        skos:prefLabel         "mass_fraction_of_atomic_chlorine_in_air"@en;
        skos:related           <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P06/current/UUUU/> .

<http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/9ZIU7TVP/>
        rdf:type               skos:Concept;
        dce:identifier         "SDN:P07::9ZIU7TVP";
        dc:date                "2018-03-13 13:56:23.0";
        dc:identifier          "SDN:P07::9ZIU7TVP";
        pav:authoredOn         "2018-03-13 13:56:23.0";
        pav:hasCurrentVersion  <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/9ZIU7TVP/1/>;
        pav:version            "1";
        void:inDataset         <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/.well-known/void>;
        owl:deprecated         "false";
        owl:sameAs             <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/standard_name/water_table_depth/> , <http://mmisw.org/ont/cf/parameter/water_table_depth>;
        owl:versionInfo        "1";
        skos:altLabel          "";
        skos:definition        "Depth is the vertical distance below the surface. The water table is the surface below which the soil is saturated with water such that all pore spaces are filled."@en;
        skos:notation          "SDN:P07::9ZIU7TVP";
        skos:note              "accepted"@en;
        skos:prefLabel         "water_table_depth"@en;
        skos:related           <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P06/current/ULAA/> .

<http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/W1SG8ALB/>
        rdf:type               skos:Concept;
        dce:identifier         "SDN:P07::W1SG8ALB";
        dc:date                "2025-05-13 08:28:42.0";
        dc:identifier          "SDN:P07::W1SG8ALB";
        pav:authoredOn         "2025-05-13 08:28:42.0";
        pav:hasCurrentVersion  <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/W1SG8ALB/2/>;
        pav:hasVersion         <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/W1SG8ALB/1/>;
        pav:version            "2";
        void:inDataset         <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/.well-known/void>;
        owl:deprecated         "false";
        owl:sameAs             <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/standard_name/snow_transport_across_line_due_to_sea_ice_dynamics/> , <http://mmisw.org/ont/cf/parameter/snow_transport_across_line_due_to_sea_ice_dynamics>;
        owl:versionInfo        "2";
        skos:altLabel          "";
        skos:definition        "Transport \"across_line\" means that which crosses a particular line on the Earth's surface; formally this means the integral along the line of the normal component of the transport. The specification of a physical process by the phrase \"due_to_\" process means that the quantity named is a single term in a sum of terms which together compose the general quantity named by omitting the phrase. \"Sea ice dynamics\" refers to advection of sea ice. \"Sea ice\" means all ice floating in the sea which has formed from freezing sea water, rather than by other processes such as calving of land ice to form icebergs. Positive and negative numbers refer to total northward/eastward and southward/westward transports, respectively."@en;
        skos:notation          "SDN:P07::W1SG8ALB";
        skos:note              "accepted"@en;
        skos:prefLabel         "snow_transport_across_line_due_to_sea_ice_dynamics"@en;
        skos:related           <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P06/current/KGPS/> .

<http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/1QS91H7N/>
        rdf:type               skos:Concept;
        dce:identifier         "SDN:P07::1QS91H7N";
        dc:date                "2013-06-27 14:07:54.0";
        dc:identifier          "SDN:P07::1QS91H7N";
        pav:authoredOn         "2013-06-27 14:07:54.0";
        pav:hasCurrentVersion  <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/1QS91H7N/1/>;
        pav:version            "1";
        void:inDataset         <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/.well-known/void>;
        owl:deprecated         "false";
        owl:sameAs             <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/standard_name/upward_heat_flux_in_sea_water_due_to_convection/> , <http://mmisw.org/ont/cf/parameter/upward_heat_flux_in_sea_water_due_to_convection>;
        owl:versionInfo        "1";
        skos:altLabel          "";
        skos:definition        "\"Upward\" indicates a vector component which is positive when directed upward (negative downward). The specification of a physical process by the phrase due_to_process means that the quantity named is a single term in a sum of terms which together compose the general quantity named by omitting the phrase. In accordance with common usage in geophysical disciplines, \"flux\" implies per unit area, called \"flux density\" in physics."@en;
        skos:notation          "SDN:P07::1QS91H7N";
        skos:note              "accepted"@en;
        skos:prefLabel         "upward_heat_flux_in_sea_water_due_to_convection"@en;
        skos:related           <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P06/current/UFAA/> .

<http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/CFSN0024/>
        rdf:type               skos:Concept;
        dce:identifier         "SDN:P07::CFSN0024";
        dc:date                "2024-05-20 20:39:49.0";
        dc:identifier          "SDN:P07::CFSN0024";
        pav:authoredOn         "2024-05-20 20:39:49.0";
        pav:hasCurrentVersion  <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/CFSN0024/2/>;
        pav:hasVersion         <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/CFSN0024/1/>;
        pav:version            "2";
        void:inDataset         <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/.well-known/void>;
        owl:deprecated         "false";
        owl:sameAs             <http://mmisw.org/ont/cf/parameter/air_temperature_anomaly> , <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/standard_name/air_temperature_anomaly/>;
        owl:versionInfo        "2";
        skos:altLabel          "";
        skos:broader           <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P02/current/CDTA/> , <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P04/current/G231/> , <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P64/current/G231/>;
        skos:definition        "\"anomaly\" means difference from climatology. Air temperature is the bulk temperature of the air, not the surface (skin) temperature. It is strongly recommended that a variable with this standard name should have the attribute units_metadata=\"temperature: difference\", meaning that it refers to temperature differences and implying that the origin of the temperature scale is irrelevant, because it is essential to know whether a temperature is on-scale or a difference in order to convert the units correctly (cf. https://cfconventions.org/cf-conventions/cf-conventions.html#temperature-units)."@en;
        skos:notation          "SDN:P07::CFSN0024";
        skos:note              "accepted"@en;
        skos:prefLabel         "air_temperature_anomaly"@en;
        skos:related           <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P06/current/UPKA/> .

<http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/VJ1PUAOC/>
        rdf:type               skos:Concept;
        dce:identifier         "SDN:P07::VJ1PUAOC";
        dc:date                "2020-03-09 15:56:07.0";
        dc:identifier          "SDN:P07::VJ1PUAOC";
        pav:authoredOn         "2020-03-09 15:56:07.0";
        pav:hasCurrentVersion  <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/VJ1PUAOC/1/>;
        pav:version            "1";
        void:inDataset         <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/.well-known/void>;
        owl:deprecated         "false";
        owl:sameAs             <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/standard_name/syntax_test_quality_flag/> , <http://mmisw.org/ont/cf/parameter/syntax_test_quality_flag>;
        owl:versionInfo        "1";
        skos:altLabel          "";
        skos:definition        "A quality flag that reports the result of the Syntax test, which checks that the data contain no indicators of flawed transmission. The linkage between the data variable and this variable is achieved using the ancillary_variables attribute. There are standard names for other specific quality tests which take the form of X_quality_flag. Quality information that does not match any of the specific quantities should be given the more general standard name of quality_flag."@en;
        skos:notation          "SDN:P07::VJ1PUAOC";
        skos:note              "accepted"@en;
        skos:prefLabel         "syntax_test_quality_flag"@en;
        skos:related           <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P06/current/UUUU/> .

<http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/7V274BWB/>
        rdf:type               skos:Concept;
        dce:identifier         "SDN:P07::7V274BWB";
        dc:date                "2023-04-24 17:39:19.0";
        dc:identifier          "SDN:P07::7V274BWB";
        pav:authoredOn         "2023-04-24 17:39:19.0";
        pav:hasCurrentVersion  <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/7V274BWB/1/>;
        pav:version            "1";
        void:inDataset         <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/.well-known/void>;
        owl:deprecated         "false";
        owl:sameAs             <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/standard_name/surface_upward_mass_flux_of_methane_due_to_emission_from_herbivorous_mammals/> , <http://mmisw.org/ont/cf/parameter/surface_upward_mass_flux_of_methane_due_to_emission_from_herbivorous_mammals>;
        owl:versionInfo        "1";
        skos:altLabel          "";
        skos:definition        "The surface called \"surface\" means the lower boundary of the atmosphere. \"Upward\" indicates a vector component which is positive when directed upward (negative downward). In accordance with common usage in geophysical disciplines, \"flux\" implies per unit area, called \"flux density\" in physics. The chemical formula for methane is CH4. The mass is the total mass of the molecules. The specification of a physical process by the phrase \"due_to_\" process means that the quantity named is a single term in a sum of terms which together compose the general quantity named by omitting the phrase. \"Emission\" means emission from a primary source located anywhere within the atmosphere, including at the lower boundary (i.e. the surface of the earth). \"Emission\" is a process entirely distinct from \"re-emission\" which is used in some standard names. Herbivores are animals that feed on vegetation. Mammals are any vertebrates within the class Mammalia. Examples of large herbivorous mammals include cows, elks, and buffalos. These animals eat grass, tree bark, aquatic vegetation, and shrubby growth. Herbivores can also be medium-sized animals such as sheep and goats, which eat shrubby vegetation and grasses. Small herbivores include rabbits, chipmunks, squirrels, and mice. The precise conditions under which herbivorous mammals produce and consume methane can vary between models."@en;
        skos:notation          "SDN:P07::7V274BWB";
        skos:note              "accepted"@en;
        skos:prefLabel         "surface_upward_mass_flux_of_methane_due_to_emission_from_herbivorous_mammals"@en;
        skos:related           <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P06/current/KSP2/> .

<http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/CF14N48/>
        rdf:type               skos:Concept;
        dce:identifier         "SDN:P07::CF14N48";
        dc:date                "2013-11-28 13:38:01.0";
        dc:identifier          "SDN:P07::CF14N48";
        dc:isReplacedBy        <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/RMOQBF66/>;
        pav:authoredOn         "2013-11-28 13:38:01.0";
        pav:hasCurrentVersion  <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/CF14N48/2/>;
        pav:hasVersion         <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/CF14N48/1/>;
        pav:version            "2";
        void:inDataset         <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/.well-known/void>;
        owl:deprecated         "true";
        owl:sameAs             <http://mmisw.org/ont/cf/parameter/net_primary_mole_productivity_of_carbon_by_diatoms> , <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/RMOQBF66/> , <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/standard_name/net_primary_mole_productivity_of_carbon_by_diatoms/>;
        owl:versionInfo        "2";
        skos:altLabel          "";
        skos:broader           <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P02/current/PPRD/>;
        skos:definition        "\"Productivity of carbon\" refers to the production of biomass expressed as the mass of carbon which it contains.  \"Productivity\" means production per unit area. Net primary productivity is the excess of gross primary productivity of organic carbon (the rate of synthesis of biomass from inorganic precursors) by autotrophs (\"producers\"), for example,  photosynthesis in phytoplankton, over the rate at which the autotrophs themselves respire some of this biomass. Diatoms are single-celled phytoplankton with an external skeleton made of silica. Phytoplankton are autotrophic prokaryotic or eukaryotic algae that live near the water surface where there is sufficient light to support photosynthesis."@en;
        skos:notation          "SDN:P07::CF14N48";
        skos:note              "deprecated"@en;
        skos:prefLabel         "net_primary_mole_productivity_of_carbon_by_diatoms"@en;
        skos:related           <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P06/current/M2MS/> .

<http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/RMOQBF66/>
        rdf:type               skos:Concept;
        dce:identifier         "SDN:P07::RMOQBF66";
        dc:date                "2018-12-17 17:17:12.0";
        dc:identifier          "SDN:P07::RMOQBF66";
        dc:replaces            <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/CF14N48/>;
        pav:authoredOn         "2018-12-17 17:17:12.0";
        pav:hasCurrentVersion  <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/RMOQBF66/2/>;
        pav:hasVersion         <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/RMOQBF66/1/>;
        pav:version            "2";
        void:inDataset         <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/.well-known/void>;
        owl:deprecated         "false";
        owl:sameAs             <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/standard_name/net_primary_mole_productivity_of_biomass_expressed_as_carbon_by_diatoms/> , <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/CF14N48/> , <http://mmisw.org/ont/cf/parameter/net_primary_mole_productivity_of_biomass_expressed_as_carbon_by_diatoms>;
        owl:versionInfo        "2";
        skos:altLabel          "";
        skos:definition        "\"Production of carbon\" means the production of biomass expressed as the mass of carbon which it contains. Net primary production is the excess of gross primary production (rate of synthesis of biomass from inorganic precursors) by autotrophs (\"producers\"), for example, photosynthesis in plants or phytoplankton, over the rate at which the autotrophs themselves respire some of this biomass. \"Productivity\" means production per unit area. The phrase \"expressed_as\" is used in the construction A_expressed_as_B, where B is a chemical constituent of A. It means that the quantity indicated by the standard name is calculated solely with respect to the B contained in A, neglecting all other chemical constituents of A. Diatoms are single-celled phytoplankton with an external skeleton made of silica. Phytoplankton are algae that grow where there is sufficient light to support photosynthesis."@en;
        skos:notation          "SDN:P07::RMOQBF66";
        skos:note              "accepted"@en;
        skos:prefLabel         "net_primary_mole_productivity_of_biomass_expressed_as_carbon_by_diatoms"@en;
        skos:related           <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P06/current/M2MS/> .

<http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/CFSN0299/>
        rdf:type               skos:Concept;
        dce:identifier         "SDN:P07::CFSN0299";
        dc:date                "2006-09-26 18:12:43.0";
        dc:identifier          "SDN:P07::CFSN0299";
        pav:authoredOn         "2006-09-26 18:12:43.0";
        pav:hasCurrentVersion  <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/CFSN0299/1/>;
        pav:version            "1";
        void:inDataset         <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/.well-known/void>;
        owl:deprecated         "false";
        owl:sameAs             <http://mmisw.org/ont/cf/parameter/surface_downward_latent_heat_flux> , <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/standard_name/surface_downward_latent_heat_flux/>;
        owl:versionInfo        "1";
        skos:altLabel          "";
        skos:broader           <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P04/current/G204/> , <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P64/current/G204/>;
        skos:definition        "The surface called 'surface' means the lower boundary of the atmosphere. 'Downward' indicates a vector component which is positive when directed downward (negative upward). The surface latent heat flux is the exchange of heat between the surface and the air on account of evaporation (including sublimation). In accordance with common usage in geophysical disciplines, 'flux' implies per unit area, called 'flux density' in physics."@en;
        skos:notation          "SDN:P07::CFSN0299";
        skos:note              "accepted"@en;
        skos:prefLabel         "surface_downward_latent_heat_flux"@en;
        skos:related           <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P06/current/UFAA/> , <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P02/current/HFLX/> .

<http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/B3FH1TYA/>
        rdf:type               skos:Concept;
