<http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/RPLADKNS/>
        rdf:type               skos:Concept;
        dce:identifier         "SDN:P07::RPLADKNS";
        dc:date                "2024-11-11 15:22:42.0";
        dc:identifier          "SDN:P07::RPLADKNS";
        pav:authoredOn         "2024-11-11 15:22:42.0";
        pav:hasCurrentVersion  <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/RPLADKNS/1/>;
        pav:version            "1";
        void:inDataset         <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/.well-known/void>;
        owl:deprecated         "false";
        owl:sameAs             <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/standard_name/tendency_of_atmosphere_mole_concentration_of_ozone_due_to_net_chemical_production/>;
        owl:versionInfo        "1";
        skos:altLabel          "";
        skos:definition        "\"Mole concentration\" means number of moles per unit volume, also called \"molarity\", and is used in the construction \"mole_concentration_of_X_in_Y\", where X is a material constituent of Y. A chemical or biological species denoted by X may be described by a single term such as \"nitrogen\" or a phrase such as \"nox_expressed_as_nitrogen\". The phrase \"tendency_of_X\" means derivative of X with respect to time. The specification of a physical process by the phrase \"due_to_\" process means that the quantity named is a single term in a sum of terms which together compose the general quantity named by omitting the phrase. \"Net chemical production\" means the net result (calculated as production minus destruction) of all chemical reactions within the medium (here, atmosphere) that produce or destroy a particular species. The chemical formula for ozone is O3. The IUPAC name for ozone is trioxygen."@en;
        skos:notation          "SDN:P07::RPLADKNS";
        skos:note              "accepted"@en;
        skos:prefLabel         "tendency_of_atmosphere_mole_concentration_of_ozone_due_to_net_chemical_production"@en;
        skos:related           <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P06/current/MM3S/> .

<http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/CFV16A23/>
        rdf:type               skos:Concept;
        dce:identifier         "SDN:P07::CFV16A23";
        dc:date                "2013-11-28 13:38:01.0";
        dc:identifier          "SDN:P07::CFV16A23";
        dc:isReplacedBy        <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/EH4D6MT4/>;
        pav:authoredOn         "2013-11-28 13:38:01.0";
        pav:hasCurrentVersion  <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/CFV16A23/3/>;
        pav:hasVersion         <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/CFV16A23/1/> , <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/CFV16A23/2/>;
        pav:version            "3";
        void:inDataset         <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/.well-known/void>;
        owl:deprecated         "true";
        owl:sameAs             <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/EH4D6MT4/> , <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/standard_name/net_primary_productivity_of_carbon_accumulated_in_roots/> , <http://mmisw.org/ont/cf/parameter/net_primary_productivity_of_carbon_accumulated_in_roots>;
        owl:versionInfo        "3";
        skos:altLabel          "";
        skos:definition        "Net primary productivity is the excess of gross_primary_productivity (rate of synthesis of biomass per unit area from inorganic precursors by autotrophs, or \"producers\", especially by photosynthesising plants using sunlight for energy) over the rate at which they themselves respire some of this biomass (plant_respiration, assuming all producers to be plants).  \"Productivity of carbon\" refers to the production of biomass expressed as the mass of carbon which it contains. \"Productivity\" means production per unit area."@en;
        skos:notation          "SDN:P07::CFV16A23";
        skos:note              "deprecated"@en;
        skos:prefLabel         "net_primary_productivity_of_carbon_accumulated_in_roots"@en;
        skos:related           <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P06/current/KSP2/> .

<http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/EH4D6MT4/>
        rdf:type               skos:Concept;
        dce:identifier         "SDN:P07::EH4D6MT4";
        dc:date                "2013-11-28 13:37:58.0";
        dc:identifier          "SDN:P07::EH4D6MT4";
        dc:replaces            <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/CFV16A23/>;
        pav:authoredOn         "2013-11-28 13:37:58.0";
        pav:hasCurrentVersion  <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/EH4D6MT4/1/>;
        pav:version            "1";
        void:inDataset         <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/.well-known/void>;
        owl:deprecated         "false";
        owl:sameAs             <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/standard_name/net_primary_productivity_of_biomass_expressed_as_carbon_accumulated_in_roots/> , <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/CFV16A23/> , <http://mmisw.org/ont/cf/parameter/net_primary_productivity_of_biomass_expressed_as_carbon_accumulated_in_roots>;
        owl:versionInfo        "1";
        skos:altLabel          "";
        skos:definition        "\"Production of carbon\" means the production of biomass expressed as the mass of carbon which it contains. Net primary production is the excess of gross primary production (rate of synthesis of biomass from inorganic precursors) by autotrophs (\"producers\"), for example, photosynthesis in plants or phytoplankton, over the rate at which the autotrophs themselves respire some of this biomass. \"Productivity\" means production per unit area. The phrase \"expressed_as\" is used in the construction A_expressed_as_B, where B is a chemical constituent of A. It means that the quantity indicated by the standard name is calculated solely with respect to the B contained in A, neglecting all other chemical constituents of A."@en;
        skos:notation          "SDN:P07::EH4D6MT4";
        skos:note              "accepted"@en;
        skos:prefLabel         "net_primary_productivity_of_biomass_expressed_as_carbon_accumulated_in_roots"@en;
        skos:related           <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P06/current/KSP2/> .

<http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/CF12N8/>
        rdf:type               skos:Concept;
        dce:identifier         "SDN:P07::CF12N8";
        dc:date                "2015-01-07 15:23:07.0";
        dc:identifier          "SDN:P07::CF12N8";
        dc:isReplacedBy        <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/FVM903KE/>;
        pav:authoredOn         "2015-01-07 15:23:07.0";
        pav:hasCurrentVersion  <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/CF12N8/2/>;
        pav:hasVersion         <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/CF12N8/1/>;
        pav:version            "2";
        void:inDataset         <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/.well-known/void>;
        owl:deprecated         "true";
        owl:sameAs             <http://mmisw.org/ont/cf/parameter/atmosphere_mass_content_of_ammonium_dry_aerosol> , <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/standard_name/atmosphere_mass_content_of_ammonium_dry_aerosol/>;
        owl:versionInfo        "2";
        skos:altLabel          "";
        skos:broader           <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P02/current/AAMN/> , <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P64/current/H316/> , <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P04/current/H316/>;
        skos:definition        "\"Content\" indicates a quantity per unit area.  The \"atmosphere content\" of a quantity refers to the vertical integral from the surface to the top of the atmosphere. For the content between specified levels in the atmosphere, standard names including content_of_atmosphere_layer are used.  \"Aerosol\" means the suspended liquid or solid particles in air (except cloud droplets).  Aerosol takes up ambient water (a process known as hygroscopic growth) depending on the relative humidity and the composition of the aerosol. \"Dry aerosol\" means aerosol without water. The chemical formula for ammonium is NH4."@en;
        skos:notation          "SDN:P07::CF12N8";
        skos:note              "deprecated"@en;
        skos:prefLabel         "atmosphere_mass_content_of_ammonium_dry_aerosol"@en;
        skos:related           <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P06/current/KMP2/> .

<http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/FVM903KE/>
        rdf:type               skos:Concept;
        dce:identifier         "SDN:P07::FVM903KE";
        dc:date                "2015-01-07 16:23:38.0";
        dc:identifier          "SDN:P07::FVM903KE";
        dc:replaces            <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/CF12N8/>;
        pav:authoredOn         "2015-01-07 16:23:38.0";
        pav:hasCurrentVersion  <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/FVM903KE/2/>;
        pav:hasVersion         <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/FVM903KE/1/>;
        pav:version            "2";
        void:inDataset         <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/.well-known/void>;
        owl:deprecated         "false";
        owl:sameAs             <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/standard_name/atmosphere_mass_content_of_ammonium_dry_aerosol_particles/> , <http://mmisw.org/ont/cf/parameter/atmosphere_mass_content_of_ammonium_dry_aerosol_particles>;
        owl:versionInfo        "2";
        skos:altLabel          "";
        skos:definition        "\"Content\" indicates a quantity per unit area. The \"atmosphere content\" of a quantity refers to the vertical integral from the surface to the top of the atmosphere. For the content between specified levels in the atmosphere, standard names including content_of_atmosphere_layer are used. The mass is the total mass of the particles. \"Aerosol\" means the system of suspended liquid or solid particles in air (except cloud droplets) and their carrier gas, the air itself. Aerosol particles take up ambient water (a process known as hygroscopic growth) depending on the relative humidity and the composition of the particles. \"Dry aerosol particles\" means aerosol particles without any water uptake. The chemical formula for ammonium is NH4."@en;
        skos:notation          "SDN:P07::FVM903KE";
        skos:note              "accepted"@en;
        skos:prefLabel         "atmosphere_mass_content_of_ammonium_dry_aerosol_particles"@en;
        skos:related           <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P06/current/KMP2/> .

<http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/IK675SDG/>
        rdf:type               skos:Concept;
        dce:identifier         "SDN:P07::IK675SDG";
        dc:date                "2015-01-07 16:23:38.0";
        dc:identifier          "SDN:P07::IK675SDG";
        dc:replaces            <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/CFV15A25/> , <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/CF12N667/>;
        pav:authoredOn         "2015-01-07 16:23:38.0";
        pav:hasCurrentVersion  <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/IK675SDG/2/>;
        pav:hasVersion         <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/IK675SDG/1/>;
        pav:version            "2";
        void:inDataset         <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/.well-known/void>;
        owl:deprecated         "false";
        owl:sameAs             <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/standard_name/tendency_of_atmosphere_mass_content_of_sulfate_dry_aerosol_particles_expressed_as_sulfur_due_to_gravitational_settling/> , <http://mmisw.org/ont/cf/parameter/tendency_of_atmosphere_mass_content_of_sulfate_dry_aerosol_particles_expressed_as_sulfur_due_to_gravitational_settling>;
        owl:versionInfo        "2";
        skos:altLabel          "";
        skos:definition        "\"Content\" indicates a quantity per unit area. The \"atmosphere content\" of a quantity refers to the vertical integral from the surface to the top of the atmosphere. For the content between specified levels in the atmosphere, standard names including content_of_atmosphere_layer are used. The phrase \"expressed_as\" is used in the construction A_expressed_as_B, where B is a chemical constituent of A. It means that the quantity indicated by the standard name is calculated solely with respect to the B contained in A, neglecting all other chemical constituents of A. \"Aerosol\" means the system of suspended liquid or solid particles in air (except cloud droplets) and their carrier gas, the air itself. Aerosol particles take up ambient water (a process known as hygroscopic growth) depending on the relative humidity and the composition of the particles. \"Dry aerosol particles\" means aerosol particles without any water uptake. The specification of a physical process by the phrase due_to_process means that the quantity named is a single term in a sum of terms which together compose the general quantity named by omitting the phrase. The sum of turbulent deposition and gravitational settling is dry deposition. \"tendency_of_X\" means derivative of X with respect to time. The chemical formula for the sulfate anion is SO4(2-)."@en;
        skos:notation          "SDN:P07::IK675SDG";
        skos:note              "accepted"@en;
        skos:prefLabel         "tendency_of_atmosphere_mass_content_of_sulfate_dry_aerosol_particles_expressed_as_sulfur_due_to_gravitational_settling"@en;
        skos:related           <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P06/current/KSP2/> .

