<http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/CF14N104/>
        rdf:type               skos:Concept;
        dce:identifier         "SDN:P07::CF14N104";
        dc:date                "2019-02-04 14:52:10.0";
        dc:identifier          "SDN:P07::CF14N104";
        pav:authoredOn         "2019-02-04 14:52:10.0";
        pav:hasCurrentVersion  <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/CF14N104/2/>;
        pav:hasVersion         <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/CF14N104/1/>;
        pav:version            "2";
        void:inDataset         <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/.well-known/void>;
        owl:deprecated         "false";
        owl:sameAs             <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/standard_name/tendency_of_ocean_mole_content_of_carbon_due_to_sedimentation/> , <http://mmisw.org/ont/cf/parameter/tendency_of_ocean_mole_content_of_carbon_due_to_sedimentation>;
        owl:versionInfo        "2";
        skos:altLabel          "";
        skos:broader           <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P02/current/CING/>;
        skos:definition        "The phrase \"tendency_of_X\" means derivative of X with respect to time. \"Content\" indicates a quantity per unit area. The \"ocean content\" of a quantity refers to the vertical integral from the surface to the bottom of the ocean. The specification of a physical process by the phrase \"due_to_\" process means that the quantity named is a single term in a sum of terms which together compose the general quantity named by omitting the phrase. \"Sedimentation\" is the sinking of particulate matter to the floor of a body of water."@en;
        skos:notation          "SDN:P07::CF14N104";
        skos:note              "accepted"@en;
        skos:prefLabel         "tendency_of_ocean_mole_content_of_carbon_due_to_sedimentation"@en;
        skos:related           <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P06/current/M2MS/> .

<http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/CF12N491/>
        rdf:type               skos:Concept;
        dce:identifier         "SDN:P07::CF12N491";
        dc:date                "2015-01-07 15:23:06.0";
        dc:identifier          "SDN:P07::CF12N491";
        dc:isReplacedBy        <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/URHC8AYC/>;
        pav:authoredOn         "2015-01-07 15:23:06.0";
        pav:hasCurrentVersion  <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/CF12N491/2/>;
        pav:hasVersion         <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/CF12N491/1/>;
        pav:version            "2";
        void:inDataset         <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/.well-known/void>;
        owl:deprecated         "true";
        owl:sameAs             <http://mmisw.org/ont/cf/parameter/mole_concentration_of_nitric_acid_trihydrate_ambient_aerosol_in_air> , <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/standard_name/mole_concentration_of_nitric_acid_trihydrate_ambient_aerosol_in_air/>;
        owl:versionInfo        "2";
        skos:altLabel          "";
        skos:broader           <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P02/current/AAMN/> , <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P64/current/H316/> , <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P04/current/H316/>;
        skos:definition        "Mole concentration means number of moles per unit volume, also called \"molarity\", and is used in the construction mole_concentration_of_X_in_Y, where X is a material constituent of Y.  A chemical species denoted by X may be described by a single term such as 'nitrogen' or a phrase such as 'nox_expressed_as_nitrogen'. \"Aerosol\" means the suspended liquid or solid particles in air (except cloud droplets).  \"Ambient aerosol\" is aerosol that has taken up ambient water through hygroscopic growth. The extent of hygroscopic growth depends on the relative humidity and the composition of the aerosol. The chemical formula for nitric acid is HNO3. Nitric acid trihydrate, sometimes referred to as NAT, is a stable crystalline substance consisting of three molecules of water to one molecule of nitric acid."@en;
        skos:notation          "SDN:P07::CF12N491";
        skos:note              "deprecated"@en;
        skos:prefLabel         "mole_concentration_of_nitric_acid_trihydrate_ambient_aerosol_in_air"@en;
        skos:related           <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P06/current/MLM3/> .

<http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/URHC8AYC/>
        rdf:type               skos:Concept;
        dce:identifier         "SDN:P07::URHC8AYC";
        dc:date                "2015-01-07 16:23:38.0";
        dc:identifier          "SDN:P07::URHC8AYC";
        dc:replaces            <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/CF12N491/>;
        pav:authoredOn         "2015-01-07 16:23:38.0";
        pav:hasCurrentVersion  <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/URHC8AYC/2/>;
        pav:hasVersion         <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/URHC8AYC/1/>;
        pav:version            "2";
        void:inDataset         <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/.well-known/void>;
        owl:deprecated         "false";
        owl:sameAs             <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/standard_name/mole_concentration_of_nitric_acid_trihydrate_ambient_aerosol_particles_in_air/> , <http://mmisw.org/ont/cf/parameter/mole_concentration_of_nitric_acid_trihydrate_ambient_aerosol_particles_in_air>;
        owl:versionInfo        "2";
        skos:altLabel          "";
        skos:definition        "Mole concentration means number of moles per unit volume, also called \"molarity\", and is used in the construction mole_concentration_of_X_in_Y, where X is a material constituent of Y. A chemical species denoted by X may be described by a single term such as \"nitrogen\" or a phrase such as \"nox_expressed_as_nitrogen\". Aerosol\" means the system of suspended liquid or solid particles in air (except cloud droplets) and their carrier gas, the air itself. \"Ambient_aerosol\" means that the aerosol is measured or modelled at the ambient state of pressure, temperature and relative humidity that exists in its immediate environment. \"Ambient aerosol particles\" are aerosol particles that have taken up ambient water through hygroscopic growth. The extent of hygroscopic growth depends on the relative humidity and the composition of the particles. The chemical formula for nitric acid is HNO3. Nitric acid trihydrate, sometimes referred to as NAT, is a stable crystalline substance consisting of three molecules of water to one molecule of nitric acid."@en;
        skos:notation          "SDN:P07::URHC8AYC";
        skos:note              "accepted"@en;
        skos:prefLabel         "mole_concentration_of_nitric_acid_trihydrate_ambient_aerosol_particles_in_air"@en;
        skos:related           <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P06/current/MLM3/> .

